# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1 has "person_signifies_distinct → human_person_signifies_principles" and "less_common_includes_more ∧ human_person_signifies_principles → ¬general_includes_principles"; aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.2 has "person_individual_substance ∧ individual_undivided_distinct → person_signifies_distinct" and "person_signifies_distinct ∧ less_common_includes_more → human_person_signifies_principles ∧ ¬general_includes_principles"; they differ when only "something may be included in the meaning of a less common term which is not included in the more common term", ""person" in general signifies the individual substance of a rational nature", "the individual is undivided in itself but distinct from others" and "in human nature "person" signifies this flesh, these bones and this soul, the individuating principles of a man, which belong to the meaning of a particular human person" are true, which the first allows; aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.3 has "person_individual_substance ∧ individual_undivided_distinct → person_signifies_distinct" and "person_signifies_distinct → human_person_signifies_principles" and "less_common_includes_more → ¬general_includes_principles"
aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if ("person" in general signifies the individual substance of a rational nature and the individual is undivided in itself but distinct from others), then "person" in any nature signifies what is distinct in that nature"; aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1 does not
aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if ("person" in general signifies the individual substance of a rational nature and the individual is undivided in itself but distinct from others), then "person" in any nature signifies what is distinct in that nature"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if something may be included in the meaning of a less common term which is not included in the more common term, then it is not so that the individuating principles (this flesh, these bones, this soul) belong to "person" in general"; aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1 does not
aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if something may be included in the meaning of a less common term which is not included in the more common term, then it is not so that the individuating principles (this flesh, these bones, this soul) belong to "person" in general"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1: argue/verdicts: valid as aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.2, aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r1.3 read it, not as the others do: which reading is right decides the verdict
